[ ================================================================ ]
[ 020 -- operand order                                             ]
[                                                                  ]
[     phoenix examples/020_operand_order.phx                       ]
[                                                                  ]
[ The one rule in this language most likely to bite you. Read this ]
[ one before writing anything.                                     ]
[ ================================================================ ]

[ For an operator that cares which side is which, the TOP of the    ]
[ stack is the LEFT operand.                                        ]
[                                                                   ]
[ To write the infix expression   a op b                            ]
[ push b first, then a.                                             ]

10 3 - print 10 printa

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     -7                                                            ]
[                                                                   ]
[ Because 3 was on top it is the left operand, so this is 3 - 10.   ]
[ Most postfix languages would say 7 here. Phoenix is deliberately  ]
[ the other way round.                                              ]

2 10 / print 10 printa

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     5                                                             ]
[                                                                   ]
[ 10 was on top, so this is 10 / 2.                                 ]

[ The reason for the choice is that a comparison then reads in the  ]
[ order you would say it out loud. All four comparisons push 1 when ]
[ they hold and 0 when they do not.                                 ]

10 3 < print 32 printa      [ 3 < 10, which holds, so 1 ]
10 3 > print 32 printa      [ 3 > 10, which does not, so 0 ]
3 3 = print 32 printa       [ 3 = 3, so 1 ]
3 3 ~ print                 [ 3 is not 3, so 0 ]
10 printa

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     1 0 1 0                                                       ]

[ Once there are variables this pays off. Writing                   ]
[                                                                   ]
[     100 x <                                                       ]
[                                                                   ]
[ reads as "x < 100", which is what it computes. The cost is that   ]
[ subtraction and division read backwards, and that cost is real -- ]
[ when a program of mine goes wrong, this is the first thing to     ]
[ check.                                                            ]
